>If the probability is 1 in 4000 then the chance of it happening after
>4000 times is EXACTLY 50%. In other words, you are on top of the bell curve
>with half of the occurances before 4000 tries and half after 4000.

	Nope.  It's an exponential curve, not a bell curve.
And, the probability of having a wand of wishing would be 63%
after 4000 tries.

	On each trial, there's 3999/4000 chance of failing to
get the WoW; after 4000 trials, the probability of having no
wand show up in any of the scenarios would be the product of
all the individual probabilities, or

	P = (3999/4000) ^4000

    (try it on your calculator; it's about 36%)
The chance that one DOES have a WoW is 100% - P = 63%.

The case of 'N=4000' is less interesting than the limit, as one
calculates 

	f(N) :==  ( (N-1)/N )^N

which (for very large N) converges to 1/e, where 'e' is the base of 
natural logarithms, 2.718281828459045235...

